// ── LAYER 2 · MOTION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The hook. 16th arpeggio with an added 9th on the tonic chords for colour —
// a bare triad arp reads as an exercise, the 9th reads as a choice.
// The filter sweep completes exactly once per loop, so the build is
// predictable: the player learns the shape of the bar without noticing.
// HARMONY FIX: this used `<...>*4`. On a slowcat, *n means n ITEMS PER
// CYCLE, so the whole 8-chord progression raced past 4x while the bass
// made one pass — verified from the events sidecar: the bass changed chord
// every cycle while this layer played the entire scale in every cycle. It
// sounded merely blurry rather than wrong only because everything is
// diatonic. The multiplier now lives INSIDE each cell, so the note count and
// rhythm are unchanged but each chord is locked to its own bar.
setcps(0.5)
stack(
  note(`<[[a4 c5 e5 b5]*4]
         [[f4 a4 c5 e5]*4]
         [[c5 e5 g5 d5]*4]
         [[g4 b4 d5 a5]*4]
         [[d5 f5 a5 e5]*4]
         [[f4 a4 c5 f5]*4]
         [[g4 b4 d5 g5]*4]
         [[a4 c5 e5 a5]*4]>`)
    .s("square")
    .lpf(sine.range(850, 3400).slow(8)).lpq(9)
    .gain(0.155).release(0.045)
    .delay(0.32).delaytime(0.1875).delayfeedback(0.33)   // dotted-8th echo
    .pan(0.42)
)
